(Bug #60 - `{f:.N}` corrupted its decimals from N=18 and spliced a literal
 i64::MIN into them from N=20. The three bands had three separate causes,
 all of them "scale the whole fraction by 10^N and convert it in one go":
 a mulsd-by-10 loop that accumulated rounding error (N=18), a 10^N carry
 threshold built with imul that wrapped negative (N=19), and cvttsd2si
 returning the SSE integer-indefinite value once the scaled fraction went
 past i64::MAX (N>=20). The same cvttsd2si took the integer part, so every
 magnitude at or beyond 2^63 printed that sentinel as its integer part too.

 Every line below is glibc's printf("%.*f") for the same double, digit for
 digit - the manual promises "N decimal places" with no bound on N
 (LANGUAGE.md:3106), and a double always has an exact finite decimal
 expansion to print them from.)

a float called 'pi' is 3.14159.

(The three bands: correct through 17, wrong from 18, a bad
 integer part at 19, i64::MIN spliced in from 20.)
Print "pi to 0: {pi:.0}".
Print "pi to 1: {pi:.1}".
Print "pi to 15: {pi:.15}".
Print "pi to 16: {pi:.16}".
Print "pi to 17: {pi:.17}".
Print "pi to 18: {pi:.18}".
Print "pi to 19: {pi:.19}".
Print "pi to 20: {pi:.20}".
Print "pi to 30: {pi:.30}".
Print "pi to 50: {pi:.50}".

(A negative keeps its sign and rounds by magnitude.)
a float called 'chill' is -3.14159.
Print "chill to 0: {chill:.0}".
Print "chill to 2: {chill:.2}".
Print "chill to 18: {chill:.18}".
Print "chill to 20: {chill:.20}".

(Zero has no digits to round, at any precision.)
a float called 'nought' is 0.0.
Print "nought to 0: {nought:.0}".
Print "nought to 1: {nought:.1}".
Print "nought to 20: {nought:.20}".

(Past 2^53 a double has no fraction left, and past 2^63 the old
 integer-part conversion returned the same i64::MIN sentinel.)
a float called 'hundred quadrillion' is 100000000000000000.0.
Print "100000000000000000 to 0: {hundred quadrillion:.0}".
Print "100000000000000000 to 2: {hundred quadrillion:.2}".
Print "100000000000000000 to 20: {hundred quadrillion:.20}".
a float called 'ten sextillion' is 10000000000000000000000.0.
Print "10000000000000000000000 to 0: {ten sextillion:.0}".
Print "10000000000000000000000 to 3: {ten sextillion:.3}".

(Rounding: up, down, and a carry that lengthens the integer part.)
a float called 'just over' is 2.675.
Print "2.675 to 2: {just over:.2}".
a float called 'nearly ten' is 9.9999.
Print "9.9999 to 3: {nearly ten:.3}".
Print "9.9999 to 0: {nearly ten:.0}".
a float called 'three quarters up' is 3.9.
Print "3.9 to 0: {three quarters up:.0}".

(An exact half - the only case where a rounding rule is visible -
 goes to the even digit, which is what printf does.)
a float called 'half' is 0.5.
Print "0.5 to 0: {half:.0}".
a float called 'two and a half' is 2.5.
Print "2.5 to 0: {two and a half:.0}".
a float called 'one and a half' is 1.5.
Print "1.5 to 0: {one and a half:.0}".
a float called 'an eighth' is 0.125.
Print "0.125 to 2: {an eighth:.2}".
a float called 'three eighths' is 0.375.
Print "0.375 to 2: {three eighths:.2}".

(Every place of the exact expansion, and then the zeros that
 follow it - the value has ended, it has not run out of accuracy.)
a float called 'a tenth' is 0.1.
Print "0.1 to 55: {a tenth:.55}".
Print "0.1 to 60: {a tenth:.60}".
